\title{Universal Approximation from a Single Exponential Feature:\\
Point Separation as the Sole Engine of Density}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-24}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We isolate the minimal primitive that forces universal approximation in the EML
(Exponential--Multiplicative--Logarithmic) programme. Working in the Banach algebra
$\CX$ of continuous real-valued functions on a compact space $X$, we prove that the
entire content of Stone--Weierstrass universal approximation reduces to a single
qualitative property---\emph{point separation}---and that a \emph{single injective
continuous feature} already supplies it. Consequently, the unital $\R$-subalgebra
generated by one injective feature $g$ is uniformly dense in $\CX$. Because the real
exponential $\exp$ is injective on $\R$ and preserves injectivity under composition,
the single exponential feature $x \mapsto e^{g(x)}$ generates a dense subalgebra for
\emph{any} injective $g$. Specializing to $X = [a,b] \subseteq \R$ with $g = \mathrm{id}$
recovers, in one line, the classical density of \emph{exponential polynomials}
$\spanop\{e^{kx} : k \in \N\}$ in $C([a,b],\R)$. We discuss the multivariate companion
result---$n$ coordinate exponential features are necessary and sufficient on compact
$K \subseteq \R^n$ because a single ridge feature is never injective for $n \ge 2$---and
the resulting principle that \emph{nonlinearity is supplied by one $\exp$ while width is
forced by domain dimension}. All results have been formalized and machine-verified with
zero remaining gaps. We close with quantitative (Jackson-type) and complexity-theoretic
conjectures connecting approximation cost to a continuous analog of Kolmogorov complexity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
The universal approximation theorems that underpin the theory of neural networks assert
that suitable parametric function classes are dense in $\CX$ for compact domains $X$. A
persistent piece of folklore attributes this density to delicate analytic properties of
particular activation functions. The present work takes the opposite view and makes it a
theorem: \emph{density is structural, not analytic}, and its sole prerequisite is the
ability to \emph{separate points}. We then identify the cheapest mechanism that achieves
point separation---a single injective feature---and instantiate it with the exponential
primitive of the EML programme.

The EML programme studies function classes generated by the three primitives of
\textbf{E}xponentiation ($x \mapsto e^x$), \textbf{M}ultiplication/addition/scaling, and
\textbf{L}ogarithm ($x \mapsto \log x$). These operations generate a flexible algebra that
contains, among others, the softplus activation $\log(1+e^x)$ and all classical exponential
polynomials. The motivating slogan of this contribution is that \emph{the syntactic
richness of the EML term algebra is almost entirely unnecessary for universality}: a single
injective generator already produces a point-separating subalgebra, and the exponential
makes that generator available canonically.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Reduction to point separation (Section~\ref{sec:sep}).} A one-line proof that
  a subalgebra of $\CX$ containing one injective continuous function separates points, and a
  corresponding statement for families.
  \item \textbf{Single-generator universality (Section~\ref{sec:single}).} For compact $X$, the
  subalgebra generated by a single injective continuous function is uniformly dense, with an
  explicit $\eps$-approximation form.
  \item \textbf{The exponential feature (Section~\ref{sec:exp}).} The exponential primitive is
  injective and propagates injectivity through composition; hence a single exponential feature
  $x \mapsto e^{g(x)}$ is universal for any injective $g$.
  \item \textbf{Classical corollary (Section~\ref{sec:icc}).} On a compact interval, exponential
  polynomials $\spanop\{e^{kx}\}$ are dense---a Weierstrass-type theorem obtained in a single line.
  \item \textbf{The dimension obstruction and width (Section~\ref{sec:dim}).} A single ridge
  exponential feature is never injective for $n \ge 2$; this forces width equal to the domain
  dimension, cleanly separating the role of nonlinearity from the role of width.
  \item \textbf{Quantitative and complexity-theoretic directions (Section~\ref{sec:future}).}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Formalization status}
Every theorem and lemma stated below has been formalized and machine-checked in a proof
assistant against a current mathematics library, with no remaining gaps, no added axioms, and
no unproven assumptions. The mathematical statements in this paper are faithful transcriptions
of the verified formal statements.

\section{Preliminaries and Notation}
Throughout, $X$ denotes a topological space; from Section~\ref{sec:single} onward $X$ is assumed
\textbf{compact}. We write $\CX$ for the space of continuous real-valued functions on $X$, equipped
with the \textbf{uniform (supremum) norm} $\norm{f} = \sup_{x \in X} |f(x)|$, which is finite for all
$f$ when $X$ is compact and makes $\CX$ a Banach algebra over $\R$.

\paragraph{Subalgebras.} A unital $\R$-subalgebra $A \subseteq \CX$ is a linear subspace containing
the constant function $1$ and closed under pointwise multiplication. For $S \subseteq \CX$, the
subalgebra \textbf{generated by} $S$, written $\adjoin_{\R}(S)$, is the smallest unital
$\R$-subalgebra containing $S$. For a singleton $S = \{g\}$,
\[
  \adjoin_{\R}(\{g\}) = \Bigl\{\, \textstyle\sum_{k=0}^{N} c_k\, g^{\,k} : N \in \N,\ c_k \in \R \,\Bigr\}.
\]

\paragraph{Topological closure.} $A^{\mathrm{top}}$ denotes the closure of $A$ in the uniform norm; it
is again a subalgebra. We say $A$ is \textbf{(uniformly) dense} if $A^{\mathrm{top}} = \top$.

\paragraph{Point separation.} $A$ \textbf{separates points} if for all $x \neq y$ in $X$ there exists
$f \in A$ with $f(x) \neq f(y)$.

\begin{theorem}[Stone--Weierstrass, subalgebra form]\label{thm:sw}
Let $X$ be a compact space and $A \subseteq \CX$ a unital $\R$-subalgebra that separates points. Then
$A^{\mathrm{top}} = \top$; equivalently, for every $f \in \CX$ and every $\eps > 0$ there exists
$g \in A$ with $\norm{g - f} < \eps$.
\end{theorem}

In the formal development this appears as \texttt{eml\_topologicalClosure\_eq\_top\_of\_sepa\-ratesPoints}
(closure form) and \texttt{eml\_exists\_uniform\_approx} ($\eps$ form). The closure-equals-top conclusion
requires only compactness of $X$, not the Hausdorff axiom; this is used in Section~\ref{sec:icc}.

\section{Point Separation from a Single Injective Generator}\label{sec:sep}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{separatesPoints\_of\_injective\_mem}]\label{thm:sepinj}
Let $A \subseteq \CX$ be a subalgebra and suppose $A$ contains a continuous function $g$ that is
injective as a map $X \to \R$. Then $A$ separates points.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $x \neq y$. Since $g$ is injective, $g(x) \neq g(y)$. As $g \in A$, the function $g$ witnesses
separation of $x$ and $y$.
\end{proof}

The proof is one line; its significance is conceptual. Point separation, which superficially appears to
demand a coordinated family of distinguishing functions, is delivered wholesale by any single
non-collapsing map.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{separatesPoints\_adjoin\_of\_separatesPoints}]\label{thm:sepfam}
Let $S \subseteq \CX$ be a set whose underlying family of functions separates points. Then
$\adjoin_{\R}(S)$ separates points.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $x \neq y$. By hypothesis there is $g \in S$ with $g(x) \neq g(y)$. Since $S \subseteq \adjoin_{\R}(S)$,
$g$ is the required witness.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Formalization note]
An initial attempt to derive Theorem~\ref{thm:sepfam} from a monotonicity lemma for separation failed
because no such lemma exists in the library version used; the proof above, which unfolds the separation
existential and invokes membership of generators, is the robust route.
\end{remark}

\section{Universality of a Single Injective Generator}\label{sec:single}
From here on $X$ is \textbf{compact}.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{adjoin\_singleton\_dense}; one injective generator is universal]\label{thm:adjdense}
Let $X$ be compact and $g \in \CX$ injective. Then $\adjoin_{\R}(\{g\})^{\mathrm{top}} = \top$, i.e. the
polynomials in $g$ are uniformly dense in $\CX$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The generator $g$ belongs to $\adjoin_{\R}(\{g\})$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sepinj} the subalgebra separates
points. Stone--Weierstrass (Theorem~\ref{thm:sw}) then gives closure equal to $\top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{adjoin\_singleton\_approx}; $\eps$-form]\label{thm:adjapprox}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:adjdense}, for every $f \in \CX$ and every $\eps > 0$ there
exists a polynomial $p(g) \in \adjoin_{\R}(\{g\})$ with
$\norm{p - f} = \sup_{x \in X}|p(x) - f(x)| < \eps$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine point separation (Theorem~\ref{thm:sepinj}) with the $\eps$-form of Stone--Weierstrass.
\end{proof}

\section{The Exponential Feature}\label{sec:exp}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{expCM}]
Let $\expC : C(\R,\R)$ denote $x \mapsto e^x$ as a continuous map. For a feature $g \in \CX$, the
\textbf{exponential feature} is the composite $\expC \circ g \in \CX$, $x \mapsto e^{g(x)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{injective\_expCM\_comp}]\label{lem:expinj}
If $g \in \CX$ is injective, then $\expC \circ g$ is injective.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $e^{g(x)} = e^{g(y)}$. Injectivity of $\exp$ gives $g(x) = g(y)$, and injectivity of $g$ gives
$x = y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{eml\_exp\_feature\_dense}; single exponential feature universality]\label{thm:expdense}
Let $X$ be compact and $g \in \CX$ injective. Then
$\adjoin_{\R}\bigl(\{\expC \circ g\}\bigr)^{\mathrm{top}} = \top$: the subalgebra generated by the single
exponential feature $x \mapsto e^{g(x)}$ is uniformly dense in $\CX$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:expinj} the feature $\expC \circ g$ is injective; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:adjdense}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{eml\_exp\_feature\_approx}; $\eps$-form]\label{thm:expapprox}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:expdense}, for every $f \in \CX$ and every $\eps > 0$ there is a
polynomial $p$ with $\sup_{x \in X}\bigl|\,p(e^{g(x)}) - f(x)\,\bigr| < \eps$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:adjapprox} to the injective feature $\expC \circ g$.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Machine-learning reading.} Theorem~\ref{thm:expdense} says that a single exponential neuron
$x \mapsto e^{g(x)}$---with any injective pre-activation $g$---followed by a polynomial read-out, is a
universal approximator on any compact domain. Width and a varied menu of activations are not required for
universality; one non-collapsing exponential unit suffices.

\section{Classical Corollary: Density of Exponential Polynomials}\label{sec:icc}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{iccCoord}]
For $a, b \in \R$, the \textbf{coordinate feature} on $[a,b]$ is the inclusion $\iota : [a,b] \hookrightarrow \R$,
$x \mapsto x$, regarded as an element of $C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{injective\_iccCoord}]\label{lem:iccinj}
The coordinate feature $\iota$ is injective.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\iota(x) = \iota(y)$ then the underlying reals coincide, so $x = y$ in the subtype $[a,b]$.
\end{proof}

Taking $g = \iota$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:expdense}, the exponential feature is $x \mapsto e^x$, whose powers
are $(e^x)^k = e^{kx}$; polynomials in $e^x$ are exactly finite linear combinations $\sum_k c_k e^{kx}$.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{exponentialPolynomials\_dense\_Icc}]\label{thm:exppolydense}
For any $a, b \in \R$, $\adjoin_{\R}(\{x \mapsto e^x\})^{\mathrm{top}} = \top$ in $C([a,b],\R)$.
Equivalently, finite real linear combinations of $\{e^{kx} : k \in \N\}$ are uniformly dense in
$C([a,b],\R)$: every continuous function on $[a,b]$ is the uniform limit of exponential polynomials.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:expdense} with Lemma~\ref{lem:iccinj}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{exponentialPolynomials\_approx\_Icc}; $\eps$-form]\label{thm:exppolyapprox}
For any $a,b$, any $f \in C([a,b],\R)$, and any $\eps > 0$, there exist $N \in \N$ and coefficients
$c_0,\dots,c_N \in \R$ with
\[
  \sup_{x \in [a,b]}\Bigl|\,\sum_{k=0}^{N} c_k\, e^{k x} - f(x)\,\Bigr| < \eps.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:expapprox} with $g = \iota$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Independent sanity check]
Theorem~\ref{thm:exppolydense} also follows from classical Weierstrass density via the analytic
diffeomorphism $u = e^x$, which maps $[a,b]$ onto $[e^a, e^b]$ and carries $\sum_k c_k e^{kx}$ to the
ordinary polynomial $\sum_k c_k u^k$. Our derivation is fully internal to the point-separation framework.
\end{remark}

\section{The Dimension Obstruction: Why Width Equals Dimension}\label{sec:dim}
A \textbf{ridge feature} on $\R^n$ is a map $x \mapsto e^{\langle w, x\rangle}$ depending on the input only
through one linear functional $\langle w, x\rangle = \sum_i w_i x_i$. For $n \ge 2$ the kernel of
$x \mapsto \langle w, x\rangle$ has dimension $n-1$; any two inputs differing by a kernel vector receive the
same ridge value.

\begin{proposition}[dimension obstruction; companion result \texttt{ridge\_not\_injective}]\label{prop:ridge}
For $n \ge 2$ and any $w \in \R^n$, the ridge feature $x \mapsto e^{\langle w,x\rangle}$ is not injective on
any set with nonempty interior. Hence no single ridge feature generates a point-separating subalgebra on a
compact $K \subseteq \R^n$ with nonempty interior.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[multivariate universality; companion result]\label{thm:multi}
On any compact $K \subseteq \R^n$, the family of $n$ coordinate exponential features
$\{x \mapsto e^{x_i} : 1 \le i \le n\}$ generates a uniformly dense subalgebra of $C(K,\R)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The map $x \mapsto (e^{x_1},\dots,e^{x_n})$ is injective, so the family separates points;
Theorem~\ref{thm:sepfam} and Stone--Weierstrass give density.
\end{proof}

Together these establish the principle: \emph{nonlinearity is supplied by a single $\exp$; width is forced by
the dimension of the domain.} This gives a first-principles explanation of why neural networks need width: a
lone ridge neuron collapses an $(n-1)$-dimensional sheet of inputs, and recovering all directions requires at
least $n$ features.

\section{Discussion and Future Directions}\label{sec:future}

\subsection{What the reduction buys}
The reduction of universality to point separation, and of point separation to a single injective generator,
demystifies universal approximation (it is structural and cheap, not analytic), decouples the two resources of
an approximator (one $\exp$ for nonlinearity, dimension-many features for width), and is minimal (it never
enumerates the syntactic $+,\times,\exp$ terms of the EML algebra).

\subsection{From qualitative density to quantitative complexity}
Density theorems guarantee that \emph{some} exponential polynomial achieves accuracy $\eps$, but not its
degree. The degree required is a measure of the intrinsic complexity of the target $f$---a continuous analog of
Kolmogorov complexity. The change of variables $u = e^x$ transports classical polynomial (Jackson/Bernstein)
error estimates into the exponential-polynomial setting; we expect the cost of $\eps$-accuracy to scale with the
modulus of continuity of $f$ (Conjecture C4 below), and more speculatively the minimal EML \emph{depth} for
$\eps$-approximation to scale like $O(K(f)/\eps)$ for an appropriate complexity functional $K$.

\subsection{Formal future directions}
\paragraph{C1 (M\"untz-type density for distinct exponential rates).} For any compact $[a,b]$ and any sequence of
distinct reals $\lambda_0,\lambda_1,\dots$ with $\{\lambda_k\}$ infinite, the linear span of
$\{x \mapsto e^{\lambda_k x}\}$ is uniformly dense in $C([a,b],\R)$. Sharper than Theorem~\ref{thm:exppolydense}
($\lambda_k = k$).

\paragraph{C2 (Exact minimal width in $\R^n$).} On every compact $K \subseteq \R^n$ with nonempty interior,
exactly $n$ exponential ridge features with linearly independent directions are necessary and sufficient for the
generated subalgebra to be dense; any family of fewer than $n$ fails to separate points.

\paragraph{C3 (Logarithmic fragment on the positive orthant).} On any compact $K \subseteq (0,\infty)$, $\log$ is
injective, so the subalgebra generated by $\log \circ (\text{coordinate})$ is dense in $C(K,\R)$. Conjecture: the
full EML term algebra restricted to compact $K \subseteq (0,\infty)^n$ is dense, with $\log$ features giving better
conditioning than $\exp$ features near $0$.

\paragraph{C4 (Quantitative Jackson-type rate).} For $f \in C([a,b],\R)$ with modulus of continuity $\omega$, the
degree-$N$ exponential-polynomial error $E_N(f) := \inf_{p \in \spanop\{e^{kx}: k \le N\}} \norm{p-f}$ satisfies
$E_N(f) \le C\,\omega(1/N)$ with $C = C(a,b)$ independent of $f$.

\paragraph{C5 (Compositional / deep EML error budget).} Quantify how stacking EML features distributes an overall
error budget across depth, relating compositional depth to achievable accuracy for a fixed number of primitives.

\section{Conclusion}
The whole of Stone--Weierstrass universal approximation, in the EML setting, rests on a single qualitative
property---point separation---delivered by one injective continuous feature. The exponential primitive makes such a
generator canonical: a single exponential feature $x \mapsto e^{g(x)}$ with injective $g$ generates a uniformly
dense subalgebra of $\CX$ on any compact $X$, and on a compact interval this is exactly the density of exponential
polynomials $\spanop\{e^{kx}\}$. In higher dimensions the impossibility of a single injective ridge feature forces
width equal to the domain dimension. All results are formally verified. The unifying lesson---\emph{separate the
points and arithmetic does the rest; nonlinearity costs one exponential, width costs the dimension}---reframes
universal approximation as a structural rather than analytic phenomenon.

